abstract_text:
[
  "In the domain of plant cell biology, cytokinesis is known to be heat-sensitive in temperate plants and heat-resilient in tropical plants. However, the comprehensive cytokinetic gene networks and the specific molecular bottlenecks dictating these differential genetic responses to heat stress remain unknown. The primary objective is to predict these molecular bottlenecks by comparing single-cell and whole-tissue omics data from heat-stressed and control plants to construct multilayer cytokinetic networks. Diverse transcriptomic, translatomic, and proteomic datasets will be integrated by performing single-layer analyses followed by the construction of a multiplex network representing inter-layer relationships. Identifying these cytokinetic bottlenecks will provide insights into plant evolutionary adaptation to heat, enabling improved predictions of ecosystem stability and the breeding of heat-resilient crop varieties."
]

rephrased_abstract:
[
  "In the domain of plant cell biology, cytokinesis is known to be heat-sensitive in temperate plants and heat-resilient in tropical plants. However, the comprehensive cytokinetic gene networks and the specific molecular bottlenecks dictating these differential genetic responses to heat stress remain unknown. The primary objective is to predict these molecular bottlenecks by comparing single-cell and whole-tissue omics data from heat-stressed and control plants to construct multilayer cytokinetic networks. Diverse transcriptomic, translatomic, and proteomic datasets will be integrated by performing single-layer analyses followed by the construction of a multiplex network representing inter-layer relationships. Identifying these cytokinetic bottlenecks will provide insights into plant evolutionary adaptation to heat, enabling improved predictions of ecosystem stability and the breeding of heat-resilient crop varieties."
]

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "In the domain of vertebrate genomics and evolutionary biology, transposable elements are recognized as drivers of species diversification that generate novel functional genomic elements. However, the functional role of these repetitive regions remains underexplored because existing short-read sequencing pipelines typically discard or ambiguously map multi-mapped reads. To determine how transposable elements generate new transcription factor binding sites, enhancers, novel transcripts, and protein-coding sequences, the approach reprocesses large-scale genomic datasets using repeat-aware pipelines, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, and probabilistic read allocation. Over 75,000 public multi-omic datasets will be integrated by intersecting repeat-aware genomic activity maps with transposable element annotations and applying tensor decomposition to identify higher-order dependencies. This research provides a significant contribution by connecting molecular-level transposable element insertions to the evolution of higher-order regulatory networks and phenotypic diversity."
}

rephrased_abstract:
{
  "abstract": "In the domain of vertebrate genomics and evolutionary biology, transposable elements are recognized as drivers of species diversification that generate novel functional genomic elements. However, the functional role of these repetitive regions remains underexplored because existing short-read sequencing pipelines typically discard or ambiguously map multi-mapped reads. To determine how transposable elements generate new transcription factor binding sites, enhancers, novel transcripts, and protein-coding sequences, the approach reprocesses large-scale genomic datasets using repeat-aware pipelines, telomere-to-telomere assemblies, and probabilistic read allocation. Over 75,000 public multi-omic datasets will be integrated by intersecting repeat-aware genomic activity maps with transposable element annotations and applying tensor decomposition to identify higher-order dependencies. This research provides a significant contribution by connecting molecular-level transposable element insertions to the evolution of higher-order regulatory networks and phenotypic diversity."
}

abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "Structural proteomics utilizes crosslinking mass spectrometry to study protein-protein interactions and protein complexes by mapping residue-level contacts. However, existing proteomics models frequently lack structural evidence, and public crosslinking mass spectrometry datasets in repositories like PRIDE remain difficult to reuse due to inconsistent formats, metadata, and processing. The objective of this study is to standardize and reprocess public datasets with modern algorithms such as Scout, and to structurally validate interactions against network predictions using AlphaFold3 computational modeling. Data synthesis will be achieved by filtering raw PRIDE data, pooling crosslink pairs, mapping them to huMAP databases, and integrating the validated interactions into the EBI Complex Portal. This research provides physical, residue-level spatial evidence to validate or discover protein complexes, thereby improving upon interaction models that lack structural confirmation."
}

rephrased_abstract:
{
  "abstract": "Structural proteomics utilizes crosslinking mass spectrometry to study protein-protein interactions and protein complexes by mapping residue-level contacts. However, existing proteomics models frequently lack structural evidence, and public crosslinking mass spectrometry datasets in repositories like PRIDE remain difficult to reuse due to inconsistent formats, metadata, and processing. The objective of this study is to standardize and reprocess public datasets with modern algorithms such as Scout, and to structurally validate interactions against network predictions using AlphaFold3 computational modeling. Data synthesis will be achieved by filtering raw PRIDE data, pooling crosslink pairs, mapping them to huMAP databases, and integrating the validated interactions into the EBI Complex Portal. This research provides physical, residue-level spatial evidence to validate or discover protein complexes, thereby improving upon interaction models that lack structural confirmation."
}

abstract_text:
[
  "In mass spectrometry-based proteomics, public repositories such as PRIDE host massive datasets, supported by structural standards like SDRF-Proteomics. However, the effective discovery and reuse of these datasets are currently hindered because metadata remains incomplete, inconsistently annotated, or buried in unstructured text. To address this limitation, the primary objective is to develop intelligent, automated workflows that extract and refine technical and biological metadata using tools such as Nextflow and fine-tuned large language models. Extracted information from raw mass spectrometry files and open-access scientific literature will be harmonized using controlled vocabularies and linked into the PRIDE repository to enable API-driven querying. Ultimately, this automated enhancement will render datasets findable, accessible, interoperable, and reusable (FAIR), facilitating large-scale secondary analyses, artificial intelligence model training, and a scalable framework for other omics disciplines."
]
]

full_text:
[
  {
    "heading": "SCIENTIFIC BACKGROUND AND RESEARCH QUESTION",
    "text": "Mass spectrometry-based proteomics suffers from incomplete, inconsistently structured, and poorly accessible metadata, which limits data findability and reuse despite the existence of public repositories like PRIDE and structural standards like SDRF-Proteomics. The primary objective is to develop intelligent, automated workflows to extract, refine, and integrate high-quality metadata from raw mass spectrometry files and scientific publications into the PRIDE repository. Enhancing metadata will make datasets findable, accessible, interoperable, and reusable, thereby enabling large-scale secondary analyses, artificial intelligence model training, automated systematic querying, and the creation of a scalable template for other omics disciplines."
  },
  {
    "heading": "METHODOLOGY AND ANALYTICAL APPROACH",
    "text": "The study employs a stepwise approach beginning with standard bottom-up proteomics to extract technical metadata from raw mass spectrometry files and biological metadata from scientific literature for integration into automated workflows. The analytical strategy utilizes the SDRF-Proteomics format, mzML conversion, Param-Medic, Nextflow, fine-tuned large language models with ensemble cross-checking, and integration into lesSDRF. Results will be validated against a curated set of public datasets with SDRF metadata acting as a gold standard and a manually curated private test set serving as an independent benchmark with scoring emphasizing precision."
  },
  {
    "heading": "DATA SOURCES AND SYNTHESIS PLAN",
    "text": "The project utilizes raw mass spectrometry datasets, public data from the PRIDE repository, open-access scientific publications, and reference data for organism identification. Extracted metadata will be harmonized using controlled vocabularies and ontologies, linked to PRIDE repository datasets, and made accessible via the PRIDE web portal and a metadata-based querying application programming interface. Missing contextual information in raw data will be addressed through natural language processing of literature, proprietary data formats will be converted to mzML, and potential natural language processing errors will be mitigated by large language model self-verification and ensemble cross-checking."
  },
  {
    "heading": "FEASIBILITY AND TIMELINE",
    "text": "The two-year timeline dedicates the first year to workflow development, metadata compilation, and the launch of a community challenge. The second year focuses on challenge evaluation, tool integration into lesSDRF, retrospective metadata generation, and the implementation of PRIDE querying capabilities."
  },
  {
    "heading": "OPEN SCIENCE AND TEAM COMPOSITION",
    "text": "The project relies on a cross-disciplinary team comprising specialists in bioinformatics, machine learning, natural language processing, proteomics, and repository management, along with NCEMS staff scientists and assistants. Open science practices will be ensured by releasing code on GitHub, preprinting manuscripts alongside data, sharing tutorial resources via ProteomicsML, and open-sourcing the winning algorithms from the community challenge."
  }
]

