full_text:
{
  "SCIENTIFIC BACKGROUND AND RESEARCH QUESTION": "The research domain focuses on single-cell genomics of the human brain to explore the relationship between three-dimensional chromatin architecture and gene expression in both healthy states and Alzheimer's disease. Current single-modality analyses provide a fragmented view of gene regulatory networks and fail to capture the functional relevance of structural motifs. The primary objective is to develop a multimodal generative artificial intelligence model that integrates chromatin accessibility, three-dimensional chromatin organization, and gene expression to identify epigenetic drivers of Alzheimer's disease progression.",
  "METHODOLOGY AND ANALYTICAL APPROACH": "The analytical strategy employs a pretraining and fine-tuning approach that uses contrastive learning adapted from the CLIP framework to align chromatin and gene expression embeddings. The methodology integrates a transformer-based architecture utilizing a Vision Transformer for single-cell Hi-C data, the EPCOT framework, scGPT, tensor decomposition, and two novel computational tools called scCOE and scGEP. Model performance will be evaluated by splitting data into training, validation, and testing subsets to benchmark the multimodal model against unimodal models.",
  "DATA SOURCES AND SYNTHESIS PLAN": "The project will utilize single-cell Hi-C, single-cell RNA-sequencing, and bulk ATAC-sequencing datasets from human brain tissues of Alzheimer's patients and healthy controls, alongside supplementary mouse cell co-assay datasets. Data synthesis will involve categorizing cells into a consensus set of cell types and aligning chromatin and gene expression embeddings via contrastive learning. The study will address the lack of human brain co-assay data by using tensor decomposition to impute missing data and integrate incomplete samples.",
  "FEASIBILITY AND TIMELINE": "Feasibility constraints regarding the lack of co-assay data for human brain cells will be mitigated through a pretraining and fine-tuning approach alongside the use of supplementary mouse cell datasets. Regarding the timeline, the proposal does not specify that information.",
  "OPEN SCIENCE AND TEAM COMPOSITION": "The project requires cross-disciplinary expertise in bioinformatics, deep learning, and neurobiology, which will be provided by a multi-lab working group, the NCEMS team, and a requested staff scientist. The investigators plan to publish two manuscripts and produce two computational tools for the scientific community, although the proposal does not specify that information regarding exact open science policies or personnel names."
}
}

