abstract_text:
{
  "abstract": "In the domain of molecular and cellular biology, organ cross-talk functions as bidirectional communication between organs to maintain physiological homeostasis and drive systemic adaptation during endurance exercise. The molecular architecture, directionality, and temporal regulation of inter-organ signaling at the whole-organism level remain largely unexplored, as current multi-organ network models rely on heterogeneous, non-homologous datasets that limit biological interpretability. The primary objective is to map molecular responses across omic layers and time to identify predictors of phenotypic adaptation and inter-organ cross-talk by creating a computational digital twin rat using a reaction-diffusion equation framework, graph Laplacian operators, and physics-informed neural networks. The integration strategy utilizes large-scale, longitudinal multi-omic datasets derived from eighteen solid tissues of rats subjected to an endurance exercise program, performing vertical intra-organ and horizontal inter-organ integration via principal component analysis and a hybrid computational framework. This work is significant because it elucidates the fundamental biology of organismal physiology and disease states through the novel creation of an active digital twin platform designed to simulate and predict systemic adaptation and dysfunction."
}

